  "topic": "Message delivery and ordering",
  "tag": "CHAT",
  "script": {
    "hook": "Messages show up twice, or out of order. Interviewers love this chat bug.",
    "question": "Design message delivery and ordering for a chat app.",
    "trap": "The trap: send over a socket and append whatever arrives. Retries create duplicates, and the network reorders messages.",
    "steps": [
      "Client ID. Give every message a client generated ID before sending. The server dedupes on it, so a retry is safe.",
      "Server order. The server assigns a sequence number per conversation. Clients sort by it, never by the device clock.",
      "Acks and retries. Track sent, delivered, and read states. Retry with backoff until the server acks.",
      "Gap repair. If sequence five follows three, ask for four. On reconnect, sync from the last seen sequence."
    ],
    "takeaway": "Client ID for dedupe, server sequence for order, and ack everything.",
    "teaser": "Tomorrow: typing indicators and presence.",
    "cta": "Comment M S D for the free cheatsheet."
  },
